{
  "name": "Exploring Media in Muslim Contexts: Satire, Social Taboos and War",
  "parent_id": 203314133681389,
  "group_id": "57be33ad-4e3d-404e-8418-2cb7ee269021",
  "alternates": [],
  "created_at": "2026-07-30T14:53:04.446Z",
  "deleted_at": null,
  "sort_by_date": null,
  "tag_list": [],
  "updated_at": "2026-08-12T15:09:53.005Z",
  "published_at": "2026-08-12T15:09:52.991Z",
  "id": 203708761933568,
  "uuid": "01fd12fe-c8e5-41e7-8c44-baad8bf41a33",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "e0577bfb-fd6c-0712-c619-2243971553f2",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "d7f9f7b4-68df-e103-5394-c9a7221cdfc8",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "With\n around 40 papers presented and over 100 participants, the conference \nbrought together academics, journalists and other media practitioners at\n the Aga Khan University's UK campus.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "A broad range of topics, \nsuch as satire online and in publications such as Charlie Hebdo was \ndiscussed; as were subjects like the representation of men versus women;\n government control of the media; and the changes in social media and \ntheir impact. The conference was both topical and insightful, exploring \nrecent and historical events in various countries with Muslim societies,\n from Kenya to Egypt and from Syria to Pakistan.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Among the many speakers, were:",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Dr\n Marwan Kraidy, author of The Naked Blogger of Cairo: Creative \nInsurgency in the Arab World Christa Salamandra with her paper 'The Past\n Progressive: History and Belonging in Syrian Social Drama' David \nHarrison from Al-Jazeera presented 'Muslims and Media Freedom: Friends \nor Foes?' drawing on his experience of training journalists abroad and \nthe obstacles faced by the media in some countries Hafez Al-Mirazi, a \nbroadcaster on numerous outlets including the BBC, Al Arabiya and Dream \nTV, presented 'Image Wars: Arab Media Dilemma Abroad and at Home' Bahaar\n Joya, a specialist on women's rights in areas of conflict and a BBC \njournalist,  spoke about how social media helped to reduce violence \nagainst and exploitation of women in a talk entitled 'Impact of Social \nMedia on Afghanistan's Social Taboos' \"Discussing and understanding how \nthe media functions in Muslim societies and in the world at large; as \nwell as the transformations in media and in society are an important \npart of our work at the ISMC,\" said Dr David Taylor, director of the \nISMC.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "\"We are in a unique position in the UK to bring together a \nbroad range of expertise and invite people to understand the \nrelationships between digital, print and broadcast media and the \nperceptions of those within and outside Muslim societies and contexts.\"",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "\"In\n western countries, particularly, there is a tendency to see Muslim \nsocieties as somehow one-dimensional - where they might stand on a scale\n of global extremism. In fact, Muslim societies are as diverse as any \nother and their media perhaps even more so. At the Aga Khan Graduate \nSchool of Media and Communications in Nairobi, we not only recognise \nthis diversity, but celebrate it,\" commented Michael Meyer, founding \ndean of the GSMC.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Recordings of each presentation can be viewed here.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLU70S6ZdtaxOK4a0y7UDRczbp_ugrX-3O",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Look at the eventprogramme, speaker details and abstracts.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
